    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(7))  # F0..F6\n    customers = list(range(6))  # C0..C5\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(1, 97), (48, 95), (45, 54), (71, 54), (2, 71), (84, 10), (53, 47)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(72, 93), (8, 8), (11, 80), (68, 10), (45, 48), (9, 19)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [137.0, 114.0, 97.0, 121.0, 137.0, 89.0, 126.0]\n    customer_demands = [32.0, 47.0, 13.0, 43.0, 17.0, 41.0]\n    fixed_costs = [136.0, 370.0, 125.0, 477.0, 422.0, 318.0, 288.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.5  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Framing the warehouse location and customer service decision\n\nTeam,\n\nWe\u2019re at the point where we need to decide which warehouse sites to open and how to route shipments so we can serve all customers reliably at the lowest overall cost. In simple terms, we\u2019re balancing the trade-off between paying to open more locations (to be closer to customers) and paying to ship farther distances from fewer locations.\n\nHere\u2019s the business context and the inputs guiding this decision:\n- Our candidate warehouse locations are specified as map coordinates in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (1, 97)\n  - F1: (48, 95)\n  - F2: (45, 54)\n  - F3: (71, 54)\n  - F4: (2, 71)\n  - F5: (84, 10)\n  - F6: (53, 47). Each potential site carries a fixed opening cost, summarized in [136.0, 370.0, 125.0, 477.0, 422.0, 318.0, 288.0], and a throughput limit captured in [137.0, 114.0, 97.0, 121.0, 137.0, 89.0, 126.0].\n- Customer delivery points are provided as map coordinates in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (72, 93)\n  - C1: (8, 8)\n  - C2: (11, 80)\n  - C3: (68, 10)\n  - C4: (45, 48)\n  - C5: (9, 19), with each customer\u2019s required volume shown in [32.0, 47.0, 13.0, 43.0, 17.0, 41.0].\n- Transportation expenses depend on how far we ship and how much we ship; the applicable shipping rate is 0.5. Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates.\n\nBusiness rules are straightforward:\n- We may open any subset of the candidate sites, but a facility can only ship if it\u2019s opened.\n- Every customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied.\n- Total shipments out of any open facility cannot exceed its capacity.\n\nOur objective is to minimize total cost\u2014the sum of the fixed costs for any warehouses we open plus the shipping costs incurred to deliver product to customers over the Euclidean distances at the stated rate. Opening more sites can shorten distances (and reduce per-unit shipping spend) but increases fixed costs; opening fewer sites keeps fixed costs down but can lead to longer hauls and potential capacity pinch points. \n\nWith the above data and rules, we will evaluate feasible configurations and recommend a network design that delivers the lowest total cost while meeting all customer demand.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Framing the warehouse location and customer service decision\n\nTeam,\n\nWe\u2019re at the point where we need to decide which warehouse sites to open and how to route shipments so we can serve all customers reliably at the lowest overall cost. In simple terms, we\u2019re balancing the trade-off between paying to open more locations (to be closer to customers) and paying to ship farther distances from fewer locations.\n\nHere\u2019s the business context and the inputs guiding this decision:\n- Our candidate warehouse locations are specified as map coordinates in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}. Each potential site carries a fixed opening cost, summarized in {FIXED_COSTS}, and a throughput limit captured in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}.\n- Customer delivery points are provided as map coordinates in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}, with each customer\u2019s required volume shown in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}.\n- Transportation expenses depend on how far we ship and how much we ship; the applicable shipping rate is {TRANSPORT_RATE}. Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates.\n\nBusiness rules are straightforward:\n- We may open any subset of the candidate sites, but a facility can only ship if it\u2019s opened.\n- Every customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied.\n- Total shipments out of any open facility cannot exceed its capacity.\n\nOur objective is to minimize total cost\u2014the sum of the fixed costs for any warehouses we open plus the shipping costs incurred to deliver product to customers over the Euclidean distances at the stated rate. Opening more sites can shorten distances (and reduce per-unit shipping spend) but increases fixed costs; opening fewer sites keeps fixed costs down but can lead to longer hauls and potential capacity pinch points. \n\nWith the above data and rules, we will evaluate feasible configurations and recommend a network design that delivers the lowest total cost while meeting all customer demand."
